        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new method landed in the wrong spot, orphaning an existing Javadoc.\n\n**Dangling/stacked Javadoc (OwnerTelephones.java:38-52)** \u2014 `toDisplay` was inserted *between* the pre-existing `toE164` Javadoc and the `toE164` method. The result is two Javadoc blocks stacked back-to-back:\n\n- Lines 38-43: Javadoc that describes E.164 *conversion* (\"Converts `telephone` to its E.164 form, or returns `null`...\") \u2014 this documents `toE164`.\n- Lines 44-51: the new Javadoc for `toDisplay`.\n- Line 52: `toDisplay`.\n- Line 73: `toE164` now sits with **no Javadoc above it at all**.\n\nSo the conversion Javadoc now visually attaches to `toDisplay` (which it does not describe), and `toE164` has lost its doc comment. A maintainer reading top-to-bottom sees a formatting method prefaced by a comment about returning `null` for bad E.164 input \u2014 actively misleading.\n\nFix: move the new `toDisplay` method + its Javadoc to *after* `toE164` (the existing/primary method), restoring the conversion Javadoc directly above `toE164`. Placing the new public formatter after the existing conversion method also reads more naturally than wedging it between the constructor and `toE164`.\n\nEverything else is fine: putting the human-formatting rule in `OwnerTelephones` (the stated single source of truth for the telephone rule, already owning `NATIONAL_DIGITS`) is the right unit, the mapper/openapi additions are coherent and additive, and widening the class to `public` to expose `toDisplay` to the mapper is reasonable and consistent with how `OwnerIdentity.identityKey` is already referenced."
